> So after using unloadNamespace() on reshape2, plyr, and stringr (because
> plyr refused to unload) , I then updated both plyr and stringr and reloaded
> reshape2:

I wouldn't expect unloadNamespace to reliably "unload" packages.  You
are much better off starting a fresh R session.

> Hadley: If you have multiple levels of dependencies between these packages,
> why leave them in a disjointed state where users will get tripped up by
> version mismatches that are not checked? It would seem that 'reshape2' and
> 'plyr' are now "joined at the hip", so why not a 'reshapeR' package? OR:
> Perhaps an update_HW_packages function that would unload everything in the
> proper order and then go out to CRAN and get the most recent package combo?

The basic problem is that while I do everything I can to prevent this,
the package versioning system in R is broken, and it's very hard to
reliably ensure that every package has all the correct dependencies in
place.  I am slowly developing better tools to detect and resolve
this, but since it's a purely software development task, I can't make
it a very high priority.

Hadley

